WEBVTT

00:00.670 --> 00:04.550
Part b-- find the slope
of the tangent line

00:04.550 --> 00:09.180
to the path of the particle
at time t is equal to 3.

00:09.180 --> 00:12.350
So the slope of the
tangent line is just

00:12.350 --> 00:15.690
going to be equal to
the rate of change of y

00:15.690 --> 00:19.330
with respect to x at that point.

00:19.330 --> 00:26.349
And this is the same
thing as dy/dt over dx/dt.

00:26.349 --> 00:28.640
And dealing with differentials
is a little bit strange,

00:28.640 --> 00:30.810
especially when you want
to deal with rigorously,

00:30.810 --> 00:33.820
but you can view them as very
little, small changes in y,

00:33.820 --> 00:35.560
small changes in,
t small changes in

00:35.560 --> 00:37.050
x, small changes in t.

00:37.050 --> 00:39.290
When you view it this way,
you could say, hey, look.

00:39.290 --> 00:42.460
If I just multiply both
sides, both the numerator

00:42.460 --> 00:45.190
and the denominator,
by dt over dt-- which,

00:45.190 --> 00:47.950
if you do view them as
the same small change--

00:47.950 --> 00:49.620
then they would
cancel out, and you

00:49.620 --> 00:52.467
would get back to
the dy over dx.

00:52.467 --> 00:54.050
And the reason why
I wrote it this way

00:54.050 --> 00:57.980
is that they actually
give us these values.

00:57.980 --> 01:01.650
They give us dy/dt and
they give us dx/dt.

01:01.650 --> 01:11.040
So if you want to find the
slope at time equals 3,

01:11.040 --> 01:16.230
we just have to find dy/dt at
time equals 3 and dx/dt at time

01:16.230 --> 01:17.710
equals 3.

01:17.710 --> 01:21.320
So dy/dt at time
equals 3-- well,

01:21.320 --> 01:23.480
this is dy/dt right over here.

01:23.480 --> 01:26.650
It's just going to be sine
of 3 squared, or sine of 9.

01:29.370 --> 01:33.423
And dx/dt at time 3, that's
just going to be-- they

01:33.423 --> 01:35.560
tell us what dx/dt is
as a function of time.

01:35.560 --> 01:37.270
4 times 3 plus 1.

01:37.270 --> 01:40.429
Well, that is equal to 13.

01:40.429 --> 01:42.220
And then we can just
get our calculator out

01:42.220 --> 01:44.030
and evaluate this.

01:44.030 --> 01:46.020
We did a lot of this work
in the first problem.

01:46.020 --> 01:47.644
But let's just actually
take the value.

01:47.644 --> 02:01.035
So we have sine of 9 divided
by 13 gets as 0.0317.

02:01.035 --> 02:01.660
And we're done.

02:01.660 --> 02:03.480
That's the slope
of the tangent line

02:03.480 --> 02:07.020
to the path of the particle
at time t is equal to 3.

02:10.070 --> 02:11.020
I think we have time.

02:11.020 --> 02:13.930
We could crank out
part c as well.

02:13.930 --> 02:17.170
Part c-- find the position
of the particle at time t

02:17.170 --> 02:19.040
is equal to 3.

02:19.040 --> 02:25.990
So they give us x prime of t,
and they give us y prime of t.

02:25.990 --> 02:27.180
This is x prime of t.

02:27.180 --> 02:28.800
This is y prime of t.

02:28.800 --> 02:34.320
We need to figure out
x of x of 3 and y of 3.

02:34.320 --> 02:40.730
So if we can, let's
write x of t and y of t

02:40.730 --> 02:43.000
and then try to
evaluate them at 3.

02:43.000 --> 02:47.050
So let's see. x of
t is going to be

02:47.050 --> 02:50.420
equal to the antiderivative
of this right over here.

02:50.420 --> 02:56.529
So the antiderivative of 4t
is going to be 2t squared.

02:56.529 --> 02:57.570
Take the derivative here.

02:57.570 --> 02:58.790
You'll just get 4t.

02:58.790 --> 03:02.620
Antiderivative of
1 is t, plus t,

03:02.620 --> 03:04.620
and then you're going to
have a plus a constant.

03:04.620 --> 03:06.870
Or essentially, we just
took the indefinite integral

03:06.870 --> 03:09.450
of this side, of 4t plus 1.

03:09.450 --> 03:10.700
So you get your constant here.

03:10.700 --> 03:11.680
Because if you take
the derivative,

03:11.680 --> 03:13.304
this constant will
obviously disappear.

03:13.304 --> 03:14.810
You'll lose that information.

03:14.810 --> 03:18.430
Lucky for us, they also gave
us this initial condition.

03:18.430 --> 03:26.130
x of 0 is 0, when t is 0,
this term is going to be 0.

03:26.130 --> 03:28.244
This term is going to be 0.

03:28.244 --> 03:29.660
And they're saying
the whole thing

03:29.660 --> 03:30.743
is going to be equal to 0.

03:30.743 --> 03:32.930
So c is going to be 0, as well.

03:32.930 --> 03:35.920
Let me do that explicitly
just so we don't get confused.

03:35.920 --> 03:41.190
So x of 0 is going to be equal
to 0 plus 0 plus c, which

03:41.190 --> 03:43.510
they're telling
us is equal to 0.

03:43.510 --> 03:45.150
So c is equal to 0.

03:45.150 --> 03:47.870
So that tells us
that x of t is going

03:47.870 --> 03:51.660
to be equal to 2t
squared plus t.

03:51.660 --> 03:55.860
So if you want to
find x of 3, you just

03:55.860 --> 03:58.050
evaluate it right over here.

03:58.050 --> 04:00.470
So if you want to
find x of 3, it's

04:00.470 --> 04:03.510
going to be 2 times 9 plus 3.

04:03.510 --> 04:07.700
2 times 9 is 18, plus 3 is 21.

04:07.700 --> 04:09.750
So x of 3 is 21.

04:09.750 --> 04:12.320
Now let's try to do y of t.

04:12.320 --> 04:14.320
I'll do it in a different color.

04:14.320 --> 04:17.060
So let me scroll over to
the right a little bit.

04:17.060 --> 04:20.829
So let's say y of t is
equal to-- so if you

04:20.829 --> 04:23.745
try to take the
antiderivative of this,

04:23.745 --> 04:26.370
this is not simple to
take the antiderivative

04:26.370 --> 04:27.674
of this right over here.

04:27.674 --> 04:29.590
It's actually very hard
to do it analytically.

04:29.590 --> 04:31.750
Not even clear if you
can do it analytically.

04:31.750 --> 04:34.180
Lucky for us, this
part of the AP exam,

04:34.180 --> 04:36.070
we are allowed to
use calculators.

04:36.070 --> 04:37.839
So we could just say
this is equal to--

04:37.839 --> 04:39.880
and this is straight from
the fundamental theorem

04:39.880 --> 04:44.520
of calculus-- y of t is equal
to the integral from 0 to t.

04:44.520 --> 04:50.000
And I'll just write it
as sine of x squared dx.

04:50.000 --> 04:53.100
I didn't want to use a t here
and then use a t, as well.

04:53.100 --> 04:54.337
That would make it confusing.

04:54.337 --> 04:56.170
So you integrate with
respect to x, and then

04:56.170 --> 04:58.350
one of the upper bound
is going to be t.

04:58.350 --> 04:59.454
And this is y of t.

04:59.454 --> 05:01.870
And of course, you're going
to have to put a constant over

05:01.870 --> 05:04.339
here, because if you took
the derivative of this,

05:04.339 --> 05:06.380
you would get y of t, and
you'll lose information

05:06.380 --> 05:07.226
from this constant.

05:07.226 --> 05:09.100
Now, we can figure out
what that constant is,

05:09.100 --> 05:11.670
because they tell
us what y of 0 is.

05:11.670 --> 05:15.600
So y of 0 is going to be equal
to the integral from 0 to 0.

05:15.600 --> 05:17.630
So this part right over
here is going to be 0.

05:17.630 --> 05:20.656
So it's going to be equal to
c, which they tell us y of 0

05:20.656 --> 05:22.820
is also equal to negative 4.

05:22.820 --> 05:27.730
So we get y of t is equal
to the integral from 0

05:27.730 --> 05:33.130
to t of sine of x
squared dx minus 4.

05:33.130 --> 05:35.330
So that's y as a function of t.

05:35.330 --> 05:39.050
And we already figured
out what x of 3 is.

05:39.050 --> 05:41.470
Now we have to figure
out what y of 3 is.

05:41.470 --> 05:45.000
y of 3 is going to be
equal to the integral

05:45.000 --> 05:51.610
from 0 to 3 of sine of x
squared dx and then minus 4.

05:51.610 --> 05:55.040
And, once again, lucky for us,
we can use calculators here.

05:55.040 --> 05:58.660
So let's get the
calculator out, and we

05:58.660 --> 06:02.075
want to go to the
catalog of functions.

06:02.075 --> 06:04.430
So I press 2nd
Custom here, which

06:04.430 --> 06:06.145
gives us this little
catalog thing.

06:06.145 --> 06:07.770
And I want to do the
definite integral.

06:07.770 --> 06:09.930
And it's actually FN INT.

06:09.930 --> 06:10.960
So let me go to the F's.

06:14.680 --> 06:15.420
FN INT.

06:15.420 --> 06:18.400
So I have to go a
little bit further down.

06:18.400 --> 06:18.999
A little bit.

06:18.999 --> 06:19.540
There you go.

06:19.540 --> 06:21.690
That's my function
that I want to use.

06:21.690 --> 06:23.820
This is a definite
integral, and then I

06:23.820 --> 06:25.490
want to evaluate the
definite integral

06:25.490 --> 06:26.780
of this right over here.

06:26.780 --> 06:32.484
So the function is going
to be sine of x squared--

06:32.484 --> 06:34.650
that's what I want to
evaluate the definite integral

06:34.650 --> 06:39.635
of-- from 0 to 3.

06:42.310 --> 06:44.500
And my variable--
I need to see what

06:44.500 --> 06:46.690
the variable of
integration is first--

06:46.690 --> 06:48.350
so my variable of
integration is x,

06:48.350 --> 06:51.880
and I'm going to go from 0 to 3.

06:51.880 --> 07:00.210
And this gives me-- let the
calculator think a little bit--

07:00.210 --> 07:04.530
0.77356, and then to
that-- so that's just

07:04.530 --> 07:05.900
this first part right over here.

07:05.900 --> 07:07.760
And then I need to subtract 4.

07:07.760 --> 07:15.020
So minus 4 gives negative
3.226, I'll go with.

07:15.020 --> 07:18.384
So this is equal
to negative 3.226.

07:18.384 --> 07:19.550
Did I write that down right?

07:19.550 --> 07:20.480
I have a bad memory.

07:20.480 --> 07:23.580
Negative 3.226, and we're done.

07:23.580 --> 07:26.850
So y of 3 is equal
to negative 3.226.

07:26.850 --> 07:29.290
So the position of
the particle at time t

07:29.290 --> 07:32.740
equals 3, if you
wanted a coordinate,

07:32.740 --> 07:36.490
it would be 21, negative 3.226.
